## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates understanding of durability guarantees, lock-free algorithms, and recovery mechanisms in persistent memory systems.

## Key Concepts
- Crash consistency and copy-on-write techniques
- Lock-free updates using compare-and-swap operations
- Epoch-based memory reclamation for concurrent access
- Persistence ordering with CLWB/SFENCE and durable pointers
- Recovery via redo log replay and pointer validation
- NVRAM-specific challenges (wear leveling, power loss) and performance considerations

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Node {
  int key;
  struct Node **forward; // Next pointers per level
  uint64_t version;       // Node version for validation
  bool marked;           // Tombstone flag for deletion
} Node;

// Lock-free insert with crash consistency
bool nvml_insert(Node **head, int key, int level) {
  Node *new_node = allocate_nv_node(key, level);
  // Populate and persist node
  clwb(new_node, sizeof(Node));
  sfence();

  for (int l = 0; l < level; l++) {
    Node *pred, *curr;
    do {
      pred = head[l];
      curr = pred->forward[l];
    } while (!validate_link(pred, curr));

    new_node->forward[l] = curr;
    if (!cas(&pred->forward[l], curr, new_node)) {
      continue; // Retry on conflict
    }
    clwb(&pred->forward[l], sizeof(Node*));
  }
  sfence();
  return true;
}
```

## Microbenchmark Plan
- **Throughput**: Measure insert/lookup operations per second under varying thread counts
- **Latency**: Track tail latency for 99th percentile operations
- **Crash Recovery**: Simulate power failures at random intervals and measure recovery time
- **Scalability**: Test performance degradation with increasing list sizes and thread contention
- **Durability Overhead**: Compare CLWB/SFENCE costs vs volatile skip list baseline

